Assumptions I'm making explicit up front (flag if wrong, easy to adjust): host app is a long-running service (not purely client-side), plugins come from five internal teams (semi-trusted, not arbitrary third parties, but still should be isolated so one team's bug/crash/leak can't take down the others), and plugins fall into a handful of categories typical for reporting tools — **data sources**, **visualizations**, **exporters**, **transforms/calculated fields**, and **scheduled/report-lifecycle hooks**. I'll use TypeScript for interfaces since it reads unambiguously as a contract regardless of your actual host language, and Node.js primitives (worker_threads/subprocess) for the sandbox illustration — swap for your runtime's equivalent (separate process + gRPC, WASM, JVM classloader isolation, etc.) if different.

