## 1. Goals and non-goals

**Goals**
- Let 5+ independent teams ship report data-sources, transforms, renderers/exporters, and UI panels without coordinating releases with the host or each other.
- Fail-isolate: a broken or malicious plugin cannot crash the host process, corrupt another plugin's state, or exfiltrate data it wasn't granted.
- Hot-reloadable in development, safely reloadable in production without a full restart.
- Versioned contract so the host can evolve without breaking every plugin on every release, and plugins can declare what they need.

**Non-goals**
- Not designing a general-purpose multi-tenant SaaS plugin marketplace (no billing, no public third-party submission flow) — this is for internal teams. The design leaves room to add that later (Section 9) but doesn't build it now.
- Not supporting plugins written in arbitrary languages in v1. Native/WASM plugins are a documented extension point (Section 5.5) but out of scope for the first release.

---

## 2. Extension points (what a plugin can contribute)

A plugin declares one or more **capabilities** it contributes. This is the taxonomy all 5 teams build against:

| Capability | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `dataSource` | Fetch raw data for a report | Salesforce connector, internal metrics API |
| `transform` | Pure function(s) over report data, composable in a pipeline | currency normalization, PII redaction |
| `renderer` | Turn processed data into a report artifact | PDF renderer, XLSX exporter |
| `panel` | UI surface embedded in the report builder | chart config panel, filter widget |
| `scheduler` | Custom trigger logic for report runs | "run after upstream ETL completes" |
| `authProvider` | Alternate auth for a data source | OAuth flow for a specific vendor |

Each capability type has its own **typed interface** in the API contract (Section 4.3). A plugin manifest lists which it implements; the registry indexes plugins by capability so the host can enumerate "all available renderers" etc.

---

## 3. Plugin manifest schema

Every plugin ships a `plugin.manifest.json` at its package root. This is the single source of truth the registry reads — no convention-based discovery of code.

### 3.1 JSON Schema

```json
{
  "$id": "https://reporting-tool.internal/schemas/plugin-manifest/v1.json",
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["id", "name", "version", "apiVersion", "engine", "entry", "capabilities", "permissions"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^[a-z0-9-]+\\.[a-z0-9-]+$",
      "description": "namespace.plugin-name — namespace is the owning team's registered slug, e.g. 'growth.salesforce-connector'"
    },
    "name": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 80 },
    "description": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 500 },
    "version": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+(-[a-zA-Z0-9.]+)?$",
      "description": "semver of the plugin itself"
    },
    "apiVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+$",
      "description": "Host Plugin API version this plugin was built against, e.g. '3.2'. Used for compatibility resolution (Section 4)."
    },
    "engine": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": ["runtime"],
      "properties": {
        "runtime": { "enum": ["node-sandboxed"] },
        "nodeRange": { "type": "string", "description": "semver range, e.g. '>=18'" }
      }
    },
    "entry": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "relative path to the compiled entrypoint module, e.g. './dist/index.js'"
    },
    "capabilities": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "required": ["type", "id", "export"],
        "properties": {
          "type": { "enum": ["dataSource", "transform", "renderer", "panel", "scheduler", "authProvider"] },
          "id": { "type": "string", "description": "capability-local id, unique within this plugin" },
          "export": { "type": "string", "description": "named export from entry module implementing this capability" },
          "displayName": { "type": "string" },
          "configSchema": { "type": "object", "description": "JSON Schema for this capability's user-facing config" }
        }
      }
    },
    "permissions": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "network": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": { "type": "string", "description": "allowed host pattern, e.g. 'api.salesforce.com' or '*.internal.corp'" }
        },
        "filesystem": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": { "enum": ["tmpScratch"] },
          "description": "plugins never get arbitrary FS access; 'tmpScratch' grants a private ephemeral temp dir"
        },
        "secrets": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": { "type": "string" },
          "description": "named secret refs from the host secret store this plugin may request, e.g. 'salesforce-oauth-client'"
        },
        "hostApis": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": { "type": "string" },
          "description": "host-provided SDK modules beyond the default core, e.g. 'reportCache.write'"
        }
      }
    },
    "dependencies": {
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": { "type": "string" },
      "description": "other plugin ids this one requires, id -> semver range"
    },
    "author": { "type": "string" },
    "team": { "type": "string", "description": "owning team slug, must match namespace prefix of id" },
    "repository": { "type": "string" },
    "signature": {
      "type": "object",
      "description": "populated by the publish pipeline, not hand-authored",
      "properties": {
        "algorithm": { "const": "ed25519" },
        "value": { "type": "string" },
        "signedBy": { "type": "string" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### 3.2 Example manifest

```json
{
  "id": "growth.salesforce-connector",
  "name": "Salesforce Data Source",
  "description": "Pulls opportunity and pipeline data from Salesforce for reports.",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "apiVersion": "3.2",
  "engine": { "runtime": "node-sandboxed", "nodeRange": ">=18" },
  "entry": "./dist/index.js",
  "capabilities": [
    {
      "type": "dataSource",
      "id": "opportunities",
      "export": "OpportunitiesDataSource",
      "displayName": "Salesforce Opportunities",
      "configSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "required": ["orgId"],
        "properties": { "orgId": { "type": "string" } }
      }
    }
  ],
  "permissions": {
    "network": ["*.salesforce.com"],
    "secrets": ["salesforce-oauth-client"],
    "hostApis": ["reportCache.read"]
  },
  "team": "growth",
  "repository": "git@github.corp:growth/sf-connector-plugin"
}
```

### 3.3 Validation rules enforced at publish time

- `id` namespace prefix must match a team the publisher is a member of (checked against an internal team directory, not self-declared).
- `apiVersion` must be a version the registry currently supports (Section 4.2).
- Every `capabilities[].export` must exist on the built entry module (checked by loading the module in the sandbox during CI, not by static analysis alone — catches typos and missing exports before publish).
- `permissions` is the ceiling: anything the plugin does at runtime outside this list is denied by the sandbox, not just flagged (Section 5).
- `signature` is stamped by the CI publish job after all above checks pass; the registry rejects unsigned or mismatched-signature packages (Section 6.4).

