# Reporting Tool Plugin Architecture

Assumptions I'm building against: Node.js/TypeScript host process, JSON manifests, internal npm-style package distribution. Flag it if your stack differs — the sandbox/IPC design (§5) is the part most sensitive to that.

## 1. Goals / non-goals

**Goals:** 5+ teams ship data-sources, transforms, renderers, UI panels without coordinating releases with the host or each other. A broken/malicious plugin can't crash the host, corrupt another plugin's state, or exfiltrate ungranted data. Hot-reloadable in dev, safely reloadable in prod without a full restart. Host can evolve without breaking every plugin on every release.

**Non-goals:** Not a public third-party marketplace (no billing, no open submission) — internal-teams only, though §9 leaves room to grow into that. Not multi-language in v1; native/WASM is a documented escalation path (§5.5), not built now.

## 2. Extension points

| Capability | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `dataSource` | Fetch raw report data | Salesforce connector |
| `transform` | Pure function(s) in a pipeline | PII redaction |
| `renderer` | Data → report artifact | PDF/XLSX exporter |
| `panel` | UI surface in report builder | chart config widget |
| `scheduler` | Custom run-trigger logic | "run after upstream ETL" |
| `authProvider` | Alternate auth for a data source | vendor OAuth flow |

Each has a typed interface in the SDK (§4.3). The registry indexes plugins by capability so the host can enumerate "all renderers," etc.

## 3. Plugin manifest schema

Every plugin ships `plugin.manifest.json` — the single source of truth the registry reads; no convention-based code discovery.

```json
{
  "$id": "https://reporting-tool.internal/schemas/plugin-manifest/v1.json",
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["id", "name", "version", "apiVersion", "engine", "entry", "capabilities", "permissions"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[a-z0-9-]+\\.[a-z0-9-]+$",
      "description": "namespace.plugin-name — namespace is the owning team's registered slug" },
    "name": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 80 },
    "description": { "type": "string", "maxLength": 500 },
    "version": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+(-[a-zA-Z0-9.]+)?$", "description": "semver of the plugin itself" },
    "apiVersion": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+$", "description": "Host API version this plugin targets, e.g. '3.2'" },
    "engine": {
      "type": "object", "required": ["runtime"],
      "properties": {
        "runtime": { "enum": ["node-sandboxed", "node-subprocess"] },
        "nodeRange": { "type": "string" }
      }
    },
    "entry": { "type": "string", "description": "relative path to compiled entrypoint, e.g. './dist/index.js'" },
    "capabilities": {
      "type": "array", "minItems": 1,
      "items": {
        "type": "object", "required": ["type", "id", "export"],
        "properties": {
          "type": { "enum": ["dataSource", "transform", "renderer", "panel", "scheduler", "authProvider"] },
          "id": { "type": "string" },
          "export": { "type": "string", "description": "named export from entry module" },
          "displayName": { "type": "string" },
          "configSchema": { "type": "object" },
          "timeoutMs": { "type": "integer", "maximum": 120000 }
        }
      }
    },
    "permissions": {
      "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "network": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" }, "description": "allowed host patterns, e.g. 'api.salesforce.com'" },
        "filesystem": { "type": "array", "items": { "enum": ["tmpScratch"] } },
        "secrets": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" }, "description": "named refs into the host secret store" },
        "hostApis": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" }, "description": "extra SDK modules, e.g. 'reportCache.write'" }
      }
    },
    "dependencies": { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": { "type": "string" }, "description": "other plugin ids -> semver range" },
    "team": { "type": "string", "description": "owning team slug; must match id's namespace prefix" },
    "repository": { "type": "string" },
    "signature": {
      "type": "object", "description": "stamped by the publish pipeline, never hand-authored",
      "properties": { "algorithm": { "const": "ed25519" }, "value": { "type": "string" }, "signedBy": { "type": "string" } }
    }
  }
}
```

Example:

```json
{
  "id": "growth.salesforce-connector",
  "name": "Salesforce Data Source",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "apiVersion": "3.2",
  "engine": { "runtime": "node-sandboxed", "nodeRange": ">=18" },
  "entry": "./dist/index.js",
  "capabilities": [{
    "type": "dataSource", "id": "opportunities", "export": "OpportunitiesDataSource",
    "configSchema": { "type": "object", "required": ["orgId"], "properties": { "orgId": { "type": "string" } } }
  }],
  "permissions": { "network": ["*.salesforce.com"], "secrets": ["salesforce-oauth-client"], "hostApis": ["reportCache.read"] },
  "team": "growth"
}
```

**Publish-time validation:** `id` namespace must match a team the publisher belongs to (checked against a team directory, not self-declared). `apiVersion` must be currently supported. Every `capabilities[].export` is verified by loading the built module in a sandbox during CI — not static analysis alone. `permissions` is a hard ceiling enforced by the sandbox at runtime, not a hint. `signature` is stamped by CI after all checks pass; unsigned/mismatched packages are rejected by the registry.

## 4. Versioned API contract

**Two independent version numbers, deliberately not conflated:** the plugin's own `version`, and the host's `apiVersion` (`major.minor`) describing the SDK shape.

**Compatibility policy:**
- Minor bumps are additive-only — never break an existing plugin.
- Host supports current major + one prior major, all minors, for ≥2 quarters after a new major ships — a real migration window, not a flag day.
- Load-time check: same major/minor ≤ host's → load. Same major, minor newer than host → refuse ("plugin requires newer host API than available"). Prior supported major → load in **compat mode** via an adapter shim, with a deprecation warning surfaced on the plugin's dashboard. Unsupported major → refuse with an explicit migration pointer.

**The contract surface** is a published package `@reporting-tool/plugin-sdk@3.x`:

```typescript
export interface DataSourceCapability<Config = unknown> {
  fetch(ctx: PluginContext<Config>, params: FetchParams): Promise<DataFrame>;
  describeSchema?(ctx: PluginContext<Config>): Promise<DataSchema>;
}
export interface TransformCapability<Config = unknown> {
  apply(ctx: PluginContext<Config>, input: DataFrame): Promise<DataFrame>;
}
export interface RendererCapability<Config = unknown> {
  render(ctx: PluginContext<Config>, input: DataFrame, opts: RenderOptions): Promise<RenderedArtifact>;
}
export interface PanelCapability<Config = unknown> {
  uiBundle: string; // host iframes it, see §5.4
  onMessage?(ctx: PluginContext<Config>, msg: PanelMessage): Promise<PanelMessage | void>;
}
export interface SchedulerCapability<Config = unknown> {
  nextRun(ctx: PluginContext<Config>, after: Date): Promise<Date | null>;
  onTrigger?(ctx: PluginContext<Config>): Promise<void>;
}

export interface PluginContext<Config> {
  config: Config;
  log: Logger;
  secrets: SecretReader;   // scoped to manifest-declared secrets
  cache: ScopedCache;      // scoped to manifest-declared hostApis
  signal: AbortSignal;     // host-enforced timeout/cancellation
}
```

`PluginContext` and each method's arguments are the *only* way plugin code touches host functionality — no ambient globals, no `require('reporting-tool-internals')`. `DataFrame`, `RenderOptions`, etc. are versioned in the same package under the same major/minor rule.

**Compat shims** live in `host/compat/v2-to-v3/`, wrapping legacy capability shapes into current ones; deleted once that major's deprecation window closes, so shim code doesn't accumulate in the main interfaces.

**Deprecation process:** RFC posted before any breaking change → new major ships alongside old (never a hard cutover) → registry dashboard shows "plugins still on deprecated API" to both platform and plugin teams → at the 2-quarter mark, old-major plugins fail to load with a hard error pointing at the RFC.

## 5. Sandboxed execution boundary

**Isolation unit:** each loaded plugin instance runs in its own `worker_threads` Worker with a fresh `vm.Context` inside it. Two layers, different threats:
- **Worker boundary** — crash/OOM/hang isolation. A plugin that throws unhandled, leaks memory, or infinite-loops can't take down the host or other plugins; the host detects it (heartbeat, below) and kills+restarts just that worker.
- **`vm.Context` boundary** — no ambient Node globals (`process`, `require`, `fs`, raw `net`) reach plugin code. Only what the host explicitly injects is in scope: `PluginContext` plus a curated, permission-filtered set of built-ins (even `fetch` is a host-provided wrapper).

This is deliberately *not* child-process isolation in v1 — internally-authored plugins are bounded-by-mistake, not hostile-by-default, and per-process overhead across many concurrent report runs isn't worth it by default. §5.5 is the escalation path for plugins that need more (native addons, subprocess spawn, genuinely untrusted code).

**Permission enforcement** happens at the injected-binding layer, not as a runtime check the plugin could route around:
- *Network*: the injected `fetch` checks the target host against the manifest allowlist before the request leaves the worker.
- *Filesystem*: no `fs` binding exists at all. `tmpScratch` grants `ctx.scratch.{read,write,list}` scoped to a per-run temp dir — never a raw path a plugin could `..`-traverse from.
- *Secrets*: `ctx.secrets.get(name)` only resolves manifest-declared names, fetched just-in-time per call (not injected wholesale at startup, limiting blast radius if a plugin accidentally logs its context).
- *Host APIs*: extra methods (e.g. `reportCache.write`) are only bound if declared under `hostApis`.

A plugin without a permission has no code path to it at all — the capability doesn't exist in its world, rather than existing and returning "denied."

**Resource limits**, re-applied on every spawn/reload:

| Limit | Default | Override |
|---|---|---|
| Wall-clock/call | 30s (dataSource/renderer), 5s (transform) | manifest `timeoutMs`, capped at 120s |
| Memory (`resourceLimits`) | 512MB | platform-team only |
| Heartbeat | ping every 10s or considered hung | fixed |

Timeout/heartbeat failure → host terminates the worker, fails that call with `PluginTimeoutError`, respawns for the *next* call (not dead for the process lifetime — see circuit breaker, §7.4).

**Panels are a different boundary** — browser context, not a worker thread. Sandboxed iframe (`sandbox="allow-scripts"`, no `allow-same-origin`), served from a plugin-content-only origin distinct from the host app's (so a compromised panel can't read host cookies/localStorage). Host↔panel talk exclusively via typed, validated `postMessage`.

**Escalation (§5.5):** manifest `engine.runtime: "node-subprocess"` spawns the plugin in a separate OS process, same `PluginContext` surface delivered over JSON-RPC instead of direct binding injection. Opt-in per plugin due to cost (process startup, no shared V8 heap) — for plugins that shell out natively or otherwise can't live inside a worker.

## 6. Plugin registry

**Responsibilities:** source of truth for which plugins/versions exist and their manifests (not code — that's in the artifact store); resolves dependency graphs and capability lookups; tracks runtime state per instance (`unloaded → loading → active → unloading`, plus `failed`); enforces the §4 compatibility policy at load time.

```typescript
interface RegistryEntry {
  manifest: PluginManifest;
  artifactRef: string;   // content-addressed pointer into the artifact store
  publishedAt: string;
  status: "active" | "deprecated" | "yanked";
  installations: InstallationRecord[];
}
interface InstallationRecord {
  environment: "dev" | "staging" | "prod";
  enabledAt: string;
  enabledBy: string;
  configOverrides?: Record<string, unknown>; // validated against configSchema
}
```

Persisted alongside other reporting-tool metadata (reuse existing datastore, don't stand up a bespoke one); the host rebuilds an in-memory `RuntimeRegistry` (`pluginId@version` → live worker handle + state) from it on startup.

**Publishing:** CI runs `reporting-tool-cli publish` — build, validate manifest, sign, upload to the same internal artifact store used for other internal packages, content-addressed by hash. Registry stores the hash, never a mutable "latest" pointer, so installs/reloads are reproducible. Publishing is immutable — fix a bug with `2.1.1`, don't overwrite `2.1.0`. `status: "yanked"` pulls a bad version from new installs without deleting history.

**Signing:** CI signs with a team-scoped Ed25519 key (5 keys total, one per team, rotated by the platform team — not per-developer keys). Registry verifies at publish; host **re-verifies on every load**, so an artifact-store compromise alone can't get unsigned code running.

**Dependencies:** `dependencies` are other plugin ids (not code imports — plugins never import each other's code directly, that would break the sandbox boundary). Topological load order; cycles rejected at publish-time validation. Version ranges resolve to the highest already-*enabled* version in that environment — the registry never auto-installs a missing dependency.

```typescript
interface PluginRegistry {
  publish(pkg: SignedPluginPackage): Promise<RegistryEntry>;
  getManifest(id: string, version?: string): Promise<PluginManifest>;
  findByCapability(type: CapabilityType): Promise<RegistryEntry[]>;
  listInstalled(environment: Environment): Promise<InstallationRecord[]>;
  enable(id: string, version: string, environment: Environment, config?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;
  disable(id: string, environment: Environment): Promise<void>;
  yank(id: string, version: string, reason: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

## 7. Lifecycle hooks

```
        publish            enable              (success)
 (none) ------> [registered] ------> [loading] ----------> [active]
                                         |                     |
                                         | (failure)           | disable / reload / shutdown
                                         v                     v
                                     [failed]  <----------  [unloading]
                                         ^                     |
                                         | (failure)           v (success)
                                         +------------ [unloaded]
```

`reload` = `unloading → unloaded → loading → active` as one host-driven operation, so it can't be interrupted mid-way by a concurrent operation.

```typescript
export interface PluginLifecycle {
  onLoad?(ctx: PluginContext<unknown>): Promise<void>;
  onUnload?(ctx: PluginContext<unknown>): Promise<void>;
  onConfigChange?(ctx: PluginContext<unknown>, newConfig: unknown): Promise<void>;
  healthCheck?(ctx: PluginContext<unknown>): Promise<{ healthy: boolean; detail?: string }>;
}
```

All optional — a stateless `transform` needs none. They matter for plugins holding resources (e.g. a `dataSource` with a connection pool: open in `onLoad`, close in `onUnload`).

- **Load**: resolve dependencies → spawn worker → inject `PluginContext` + filtered bindings → call `onLoad` (same timeout policy as capability calls) → success: `active`, capabilities registered; failure/timeout: `failed`, worker terminated, error surfaced to the caller (not swallowed).
- **Unload**: stop routing new calls → let in-flight calls finish (bounded) → call `onUnload` → terminate worker → `unloaded`.
- **Reload**: load the *new* version fully through `onLoad` **before** unloading the old one, then atomically swap the routing table, then unload old — near-zero-downtime by design (load-before-unload, not the reverse, specifically to avoid a routing gap). If a plugin holds an exclusive external resource across versions (e.g. a webhook binding), that's the plugin's `onLoad` to handle — documented in the author guide.
- **Config change**: `enable` with new `configOverrides` on an active plugin calls `onConfigChange` if exported; otherwise falls back to full reload.

**Failure/restart policy:** a failing call surfaces a typed error (`PluginTimeoutError` / `PluginRuntimeError` / `PluginPermissionError`) to its caller — a report run reports "renderer plugin X failed," not a hang. Worker isn't permanently killed on one failure; it's respawned lazily on next call, up to a **circuit breaker** (5 failures/60s → `failed` state, alerts, stops accepting calls) rather than hot-looping restarts against something systematically broken. `failed` requires explicit re-`enable` — no silent auto-recovery that could mask a real bug.

**Observability:** every transition/call emits a structured event (`plugin.load`, `plugin.call`, `plugin.failed`) with `pluginId`, `version`, `environment`, `durationMs`, `outcome`, routed through the existing logging/metrics pipeline (reuse, don't build plugin-specific infra). Backs the deprecation dashboard (§4) and per-team health dashboards.

## 8. Developer workflow

- **Scaffold**: `reporting-tool-cli create-plugin <namespace>.<name> --capability dataSource` generates a manifest, a minimal implementation against the current SDK, and a test harness — keeps 5 teams from hand-writing manifests and drifting from the schema.
- **Local dev loop**: `reporting-tool-cli dev` watches `dist/`, triggers the same production `reload` sequence against a local-only registry entry. Same code path as prod — teams debug real lifecycle-hook behavior while iterating, not a special-cased dev shortcut.
- **Test harness**: `@reporting-tool/plugin-test-kit` provides an in-process fake `PluginContext` (in-memory secrets/cache/scratch, a permission-enforcing fake `fetch`) so unit tests exercise the same boundary semantics as production without a real worker — including negative tests like asserting a call throws `PermissionError` for an undeclared host.
- **CI gate before publish**: schema validation → build → load in a throwaway sandbox to confirm every declared export resolves and `onLoad`/`healthCheck` succeed against fake context → sign → publish. Nothing that fails a step reaches the artifact store.

## 9. Cross-team governance

- Namespacing enforced at publish (§3) — a team can only publish under its own prefix.
- `enable` is per-environment; `staging`/`prod` promotion for anything beyond a patch bump goes through the same change-review process other host-facing config changes get — reuse it, don't invent a plugin-specific one.
- `findByCapability` can return multiple plugins of the same type (e.g. two renderers) — the registry enumerates, the report-builder UI is what lets a user pick.
- Shared secrets/`hostApis` across teams are fine — permissions are declared per-plugin, not exclusively owned.
- **Deliberately deferred**: if this grows beyond 5 internal teams into a broader/external ecosystem, revisit the signing model (team keys → real CA), the review gate (manual → automated policy checks at scale), and default to process isolation (§5.5) rather than worker-threads for untrusted code. Not building it now; noted so v1 doesn't quietly foreclose it.

## 10. What ships in v1

1. `@reporting-tool/plugin-sdk@3.x` — versioned interfaces (§4.3)
2. Manifest JSON Schema + CLI validator (§3)
3. Registry service — persistence, artifact store integration, signing, dependency resolution (§6)
4. Host runtime — worker+VM sandbox, permission-enforcing bindings, resource limits, lifecycle sequencing, circuit breaker (§5, §7)
5. `reporting-tool-cli` — scaffold, dev loop, publish pipeline (§8)
6. `@reporting-tool/plugin-test-kit` (§8)
7. Admin surface — enable/disable/yank, deprecation dashboard (§6, §4)

**Open items before kickoff:** confirm which existing artifact store and CI secret store to reuse (§6), and whether any of the 5 teams' first plugins need `node-subprocess` isolation (§5.5) instead of the default — that changes their week-1 scaffold choice.

