## 4. Plugin Registry

The registry is the system of record for "what plugins exist, in what versions, in what state, with what grants." It is deliberately separate from the *runtime* (Plugin Manager, §5), so registry data survives host restarts and can be queried/administered without any plugin code running.

### 4.1 Responsibilities

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| Discovery | Watches configured sources (internal artifact registry, local dir for dev) for new manifests/packages. |
| Validation | Runs manifest schema + signature + ACL checks (§3.3) before admitting a package. |
| Dependency resolution | Builds a DAG across plugin `dependencies`; topologically sorts load order; rejects cycles; picks versions satisfying all ranges (or reports conflict). |
| State store | Persists install/enable/disable/pin state per environment (dev/staging/prod) and, if needed later, per tenant. |
| Permission ledger | Tracks granted permissions per plugin version; surfaces pending re-approval requests on upgrade. |
| Audit log | Append-only log of every registry mutation: install, enable, disable, permission grant, version pin, uninstall — actor, timestamp, reason. |
| Health tracking | Aggregates crash/error signals from the Plugin Manager; auto-quarantines plugins that trip the circuit breaker (§5.4). |

### 4.2 Data model (conceptual)

```
PluginPackage      { id, version, manifest (jsonb), artifactUri, signatureValid, publishedAt }
PluginInstall      { id, version, environment, state, enabled, pinnedVersion?, grantedPermissions (jsonb) }
PluginHealthStat   { id, version, environment, crashCount, lastError, windowStartedAt }
RegistryAuditEvent { id, actor, action, targetPluginId, targetVersion, payload (jsonb), at }
```

`PluginInstall.state` is the lifecycle FSM state from §5, persisted so the Plugin Manager can reconcile actual runtime state to desired state on startup (i.e., the registry is declarative/desired-state, the manager is the reconciler — a standard control-loop pattern).

### 4.3 Registry API (used by admin UI, CLI, and the Plugin Manager)

```ts
interface PluginRegistry {
  publish(pkg: PluginPackageArtifact): Promise<PublishResult>;         // validates + admits
  resolve(env: Environment): Promise<ResolvedPluginGraph>;             // dependency-resolved load order
  install(pluginId: string, version: string, env: Environment): Promise<void>;
  uninstall(pluginId: string, env: Environment): Promise<void>;
  enable(pluginId: string, env: Environment): Promise<void>;
  disable(pluginId: string, env: Environment, reason: string): Promise<void>;
  pin(pluginId: string, version: string, env: Environment): Promise<void>; // block auto-upgrade
  approvePermissions(pluginId: string, version: string, approver: string): Promise<void>;
  getInstalled(env: Environment): Promise<PluginInstall[]>;
  getAuditLog(filter: AuditFilter): Promise<RegistryAuditEvent[]>;
}
```

### 4.4 Sources & promotion flow

```
Team CI (build+sign) ──▶ Artifact Store (immutable, content-addressed) ──▶ Registry.publish()
                                                                                │
                              dev (auto-install on publish, relaxed sandbox)   │
                                       │                                       │
                              staging (manual promote, full sandbox)  ◀────────┘
                                       │
                              prod (manual promote + permission re-approval if changed)
```

Promotion between environments is a registry action (`install` targeting a different `env`), never a rebuild — the exact signed artifact that passed staging is what reaches prod.

