## 4. Versioned API contract

### 4.1 What "the contract" is

The host publishes an npm package, `@reporting-tool/plugin-sdk`, containing:
- TypeScript interfaces for every extension point (`DataSourceProvider`, `ReportRenderer`, `ExportFormatter`, `WidgetProvider`, `ScheduledJob`).
- The `HostAPI` interface — the only surface a plugin can call into the host through.
- The `PluginLifecycle` interface (§5).
- A `defineePlugin()` helper for type-safe manifests + entry wiring.

Plugins depend on this package at build time; at runtime they never import it directly (the sandbox injects a version-matched stub, §6.3) — this prevents a plugin from bundling a stale SDK and bypassing the host's actual capabilities.

### 4.2 SemVer discipline on the API itself

The `HostAPI` surface versions independently from the host application's own release version.

| Change type | Example | API version bump |
|---|---|---|
| Add optional field/method | new optional `HostAPI.reports.getMetadata()` | MINOR |
| Add new extension point | `contributes.dashboardTabs` | MINOR |
| Fix bug in existing method's behavior without changing signature | — | PATCH |
| Remove/rename a method or field | — | MAJOR |
| Change a method's required parameters or return shape | — | MAJOR |
| Change semantics of an existing call without signature change | risky — treat as MAJOR | MAJOR |

### 4.3 Support window

- The host runs **N and N-1 major API versions concurrently** via adapter shims (thin translation layers registered in `HostAPI` version registry). A plugin declaring `apiVersion: "^2.x"` still works after the host ships API v3, until v1 is formally retired.
- **Deprecation policy**: a major version is announced deprecated at least **one full quarter** before removal. Deprecation warnings surface in the registry's install/health UI and in plugin CI (see §9.3).
- Each of the 5 teams gets a machine-readable `deprecations.json` feed to wire into their own CI so a deprecated API call fails their build, not just a runtime warning.

### 4.4 Extension point interfaces (excerpt)

```ts
// @reporting-tool/plugin-sdk

export interface DataSourceProvider {
  id: string;
  connect(config: unknown, ctx: PluginContext): Promise<DataSourceHandle>;
  query(handle: DataSourceHandle, query: ReportQuery): Promise<ReportDataset>;
  describeSchema(handle: DataSourceHandle): Promise<SchemaDescriptor>;
  disconnect(handle: DataSourceHandle): Promise<void>;
}

export interface ReportRenderer {
  id: string;
  reportTypes: string[];
  render(dataset: ReportDataset, opts: RenderOptions, ctx: PluginContext): Promise<RenderedOutput>;
}

export interface ExportFormatter {
  id: string;
  format: string; // 'pdf' | 'xlsx' | 'csv' | custom
  export(report: RenderedOutput, ctx: PluginContext): Promise<Buffer>;
}

export interface ScheduledJob {
  id: string;
  run(ctx: PluginContext): Promise<void>;
}

// The ONLY way a plugin talks to the host.
export interface HostAPI {
  reports: {
    getMetadata(reportId: string): Promise<ReportMetadata>;
    listAccessible(): Promise<ReportMetadata[]>;
  };
  dataSources: {
    listConfigured(): Promise<DataSourceConfig[]>;
  };
  storage: {
    // Namespaced to this plugin's id automatically; no cross-plugin access possible.
    get(key: string): Promise<unknown>;
    set(key: string, value: unknown): Promise<void>;
  };
  secrets: {
    read(name: string): Promise<string>; // only names under plugin's own namespace resolve
  };
  net: {
    fetch(url: string, opts?: FetchOptions): Promise<Response>; // only allowlisted hosts resolve
  };
  logger: {
    info(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): void;
    error(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): void;
  };
}
```

Every method on `HostAPI` is permission-gated: the gateway checks the calling plugin's manifest `permissions` array before executing, regardless of what the plugin's local TS types imply (types are a DX convenience, not the enforcement boundary — see §6.4).

